{
  "abstract": "Fix $0<r_0<r_1<1$ and prescribe an integer displacement $d$ with $2^{r_0n}\\le |d|\\le 2^{r_1n}$. For a proportion $1-O(n^{-\\delta})$ of original sources $x$ on the shell $[2^n,2^{n+1})$, the first-passage order of $x$ and $x+d$ repeatedly reverses as the threshold decreases. Both initial orientations occur on source-selected real intervals containing powers of two. The number of alternating comparisons and their clock gaps grow as $\\exp(\\theta(\\ln\\ln n)^2)$ with separate positive exponents whose sum is below $1/(2\\ln 2)$. The signs agree on the shortcut and ordinary clocks, and each inclusive passage prefix misses the other root's entire forward orbit. All shortcut passages occur by $L+\\lfloor L^\\xi\\rfloor$, where $L=\\lceil\\log_2(2+|d|)\\rceil$ and $0<\\xi<1$ is fixed. The proof selects a long common parity word at equal odd counts and applies a stopped recursion to its residual pair. The scalar estimate is uniform for integer slacks $1\\le W\\le L$. By $L+W$ the count of departures from equal odd counts is at least $f_\\theta(W)=\\exp(\\theta(\\ln\\ln W)^2)$ for $W\\ge3$, outside probability $O(W^{-\\delta})$, for any fixed $0<\\theta<1/(2\\ln 2)$.\n\nFor dyadic displacements the intervals lie near the shared-prefix landing scale in a sharper clock window. Adjacent starts inherit the comparisons by counted fusion. A prescribed height band of diverging logarithmic width $W=o(\\sqrt b)$ at the shared clock $b$ has a uniform local Gaussian landing estimate and conditional reversal probability $1-O(W^{-c})$. At a prescribed central floor the probability of passage by $b$ has a Gaussian cumulative distribution with uniform error $O(b^{-1/2})$. Separate late populations have gaps of order at least $b^{1/4}$ throughout a fixed central band. Eleven finite CPU replays check the arithmetic, stopping rules, and physical passage examples.",
